---
/**
 * Página "A closer look" — NEXUS contado desde TRES problemas propios que se
 * resuelven juntos:
 *   (1) no poder recordar todo lo estudiado → lo arregla el vault.
 *   (2) perder tiempo planificando / reestructurar a mitad → lo arregla el
 *       método (axiomas, gates, decision log).
 *   (3) agentes de IA escribiendo dentro del registro → lo arregla la capa de
 *       gobernanza (permisos, buzón, integrador único, derecho de corte).
 * El (3) es el centro de gravedad: es lo que un PI tiene encima de la mesa hoy
 * y lo que distingue el perfil. La IA no aparece como amenaza ni como adorno,
 * sino como colaborador con permisos de escritura acotados.
 *
 * Figuras: las estructuras se DIBUJAN (GovernanceLoop, PermissionTable,
 * NexusLayers) porque son contenido del sitio y se editan en segundos cuando el
 * método se mueve — Charter §5, las vistas se generan. Solo quedan dos
 * fotografías, las que no se pueden redibujar: el grafo y un decision log real.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import NexusLayers from "../../components/NexusLayers.astro";
import GovernanceLoop from "../../components/GovernanceLoop.astro";
import PermissionTable from "../../components/PermissionTable.astro";
import { withBase } from "../../utils/url";

const graphFig = {
  img: "/how/graph.png",
  alt: "Knowledge graph of the vault: hundreds of notes linked into one connected network.",
  caption: "Notes linked into one navigable graph",
};

const logFig = {
  img: "/how/closer/decision-log.png",
  alt: "A decision log table: numbered decisions with their justification and date, one of them struck through and marked as replaced by a later decision.",
  caption: "A superseded decision stays on the record, struck through — never deleted",
};
---

<Base
  title="A closer look — how I work"
  description="NEXUS: a knowledge base, a plan-before-you-build method, and the write-permission rules that let AI agents do real work without clouding the record."
>
  <article class="closer">
    <div class="container closer__wrap">
      <a class="closer__back" href={withBase("/#how-title")}>← Back to how I work</a>

      <header class="closer__header">
        <span class="mono-label">How I work · a closer look</span>
        <h1>NEXUS: a research record that AI can write into</h1>
        <p class="closer__lede">
          Three things slowed me down. I couldn't hold everything I studied in
          my head. I lost time to planning itself — over-thinking it in the
          abstract, or tearing up half-built work to restructure mid-way. And
          then AI agents started doing real work inside my projects, writing
          files I would have to trust months later. NEXUS is what I built for
          all three: a knowledge base, a way of working, and a set of rules that
          decide what a machine is allowed to write.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="closer__walk">
        <NexusLayers />

        <!-- Problem one — memory -->
        <section class="closer__phase">
          <h2 class="closer__phase-title">A system that remembers for me</h2>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            I stopped relying on memory. Everything I learn — my whole
            data-science master's — lives in NEXUS as linked notes across eight
            domains: one growing graph I can always return to, instead of facts
            I have to keep in my head. Theory stays separate from any single
            project; the vault is knowledge, projects are where it gets applied.
          </p>
          <figure class="closer__fig closer__fig--solo">
            <a
              href={withBase(graphFig.img)}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              aria-label={`Open full-size: ${graphFig.caption}`}
            >
              <img src={withBase(graphFig.img)} alt={graphFig.alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            </a>
            <figcaption class="mono-label">{graphFig.caption}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <!-- Problem two — planning -->
        <section class="closer__phase">
          <h2 class="closer__phase-title">A way of working that plans before it builds</h2>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            My second problem was planning. I'd either over-plan in the abstract
            or, worse, tear up half-built work to restructure it mid-way. So
            every project now gets its own architecture, fixed axioms and a
            gated plan — shaped to it, not forced through a template. I
            front-load the thinking once, in a structure I trust, and I don't
            start building until the shape is clear.
          </p>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            The rules are the ones I'd want in any lab: every decision logged
            with its reasoning, every step gated before the next, and the source
            of truth in plain markdown I own, with dashboards generated from it
            rather than kept by hand.
          </p>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            Each project's planning layer — definition, architecture, living
            state, decision log — sits in the vault beside the knowledge it
            draws on, while the code lives in the project's own repository. They
            are split by <em>owner</em>, not by how fast they change: the code
            can have collaborators, the record has one author.
          </p>
          <figure class="closer__fig closer__fig--solo">
            <a
              href={withBase(logFig.img)}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
              aria-label={`Open full-size: ${logFig.caption}`}
            >
              <img src={withBase(logFig.img)} alt={logFig.alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            </a>
            <figcaption class="mono-label">{logFig.caption}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <!-- Problem three — governance (the centrepiece) -->
        <section class="closer__phase">
          <h2 class="closer__phase-title">Rules for machines that write</h2>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            Then the third problem arrived, and it's the one I'd argue matters
            most now. AI agents don't just answer questions — they execute. They
            refactor, sweep hundreds of files, and write into the same record I
            rely on. An agent that tidies up a decision log has quietly
            destroyed the evidence that log existed to hold, and nothing in the
            diff will look alarming.
          </p>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            So I stopped treating AI as an assistant and started treating it as
            a collaborator that needs write permissions. Everything below is a
            rule I actually run, every day, across my thesis and this site.
          </p>

          <GovernanceLoop />

          <h3 class="closer__rule-title">Write access is decided per artifact, not per session</h3>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            Every kind of file has exactly one owner. The agent is the sole
            author of code and tooling — that is genuinely its job, and I don't
            pretend to review every line. It is never the author of knowledge or
            method. Between the two sits a class of files that are generated:
            nobody edits those by hand, a script rebuilds them from the source.
          </p>

          <PermissionTable />

          <h3 class="closer__rule-title">Proposals go to a mailbox, not into the file</h3>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            When an agent concludes a structural document should change — and it
            is often right — it doesn't change it. It leaves a signed, dated
            entry in a mailbox, drafted in the target document's own style, with
            a destination drawn from a closed vocabulary so triage stays
            scriptable. Then I integrate it, or I don't. Either way the proposal
            and its author survive in the log.
          </p>
          <pre class="closer__sample"><code>### [pending] → charter §5 — Agents commit to a branch, never to the stable one
· (claude-code, 2026-07-29)

A branch is what makes undoing an entire session cheap. Reverting commit by
commit is the wrong granularity when a single pass touches ten files.</code></pre>

          <h3 class="closer__rule-title">The agent is required to refuse</h3>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            Whoever plans the work is reading a conversation; whoever executes it
            is reading the actual repository. Only one of them can see the real
            state. So the executing agent holds a cutoff right, and it isn't a
            courtesy — it's an obligation: if a task's premise doesn't survive
            contact with the repo, it has to stop and say so instead of carrying
            out an instruction it can demonstrate is wrong. The disagreement
            goes back to the mailbox, with a file and a line.
          </p>
          <blockquote class="closer__quote">
            Executing an instruction you know is incoherent is the failure.
            Refusing isn't.
          </blockquote>

          <h3 class="closer__rule-title">What version control can't record</h3>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            Git tells me what changed and when. It doesn't tell me why, who
            originated the change, or — the part with no equivalent anywhere —
            what was considered and discarded. A rejected approach leaves no
            trace in a diff, because it was never written down. That gap is what
            the logs exist to fill, and it's the whole difference between a
            repository and a record.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Self-consistency -->
        <section class="closer__phase">
          <h2 class="closer__phase-title">One rule bites on this page</h2>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            Views are generated, never hand-maintained — so the diagrams above
            are drawn from the same design tokens as the rest of the site and
            take seconds to correct when the method moves. The only photographs
            left are the two things that can't be redrawn: the knowledge graph,
            and a real decision log. A screenshot of a living system is a view
            somebody has to remember to update, and I'd rather not rely on
            remembering.
          </p>
        </section>

        <!-- Trajectory -->
        <section class="closer__phase">
          <h2 class="closer__phase-title">Built to scale</h2>
          <p class="closer__phase-intro">
            New domains, new projects, the same three fixes — and, in time, a
            consultable knowledge base over the whole vault. The system that
            organized a master's is how I'd approach doctoral research. The
            governance layer is the part I'd expect to matter most in a group,
            where the question is no longer whether people will use AI, but
            whether anyone can reconstruct what it did.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <p class="closer__outro">
        AI accelerates the work. The system that keeps it accountable is the
        part I built — and the part I'd bring to a lab.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .closer {
    padding-block: var(--space-xl) var(--space-2xl);
  }
  .closer__wrap {
    max-width: 54rem;
  }
  .closer__back {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .closer__header {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    border-bottom: var(--border);
  }
  .closer__header h1 {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
  }
  .closer__lede {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .closer__walk {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2xl);
  }
  /* Los grid items traen min-width:auto y no encogen por debajo de su
     contenido: en móvil eso empujaba los SVG y la tabla a ~546px y hacía
     scrollear la página entera en horizontal. Con min-width:0 encogen y el
     scroll queda donde debe, dentro de .perm__scroll. */
  .closer__walk > * {
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .closer__phase-title {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 30%, transparent);
  }
  .closer__phase-intro {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
  }
  .closer__phase-intro:last-of-type {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
  }
  .closer__phase-intro code {
    background: var(--paper-2);
    padding: 0.05em 0.35em;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  /* Sub-encabezado dentro de la sección de gobernanza: una regla, un bloque. */
  .closer__rule-title {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
  }

  .closer__fig {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .closer__fig--solo {
    max-width: 38rem;
  }
  .closer__fig a {
    display: block;
    border: var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--paper-2);
    transition: border-color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      box-shadow var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }
  .closer__fig a:hover {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 55%, var(--line));
    box-shadow: 0 6px 24px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.08);
  }
  .closer__fig img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .closer__fig figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  /* Entrada de buzón real, en el formato en que se escribe. */
  .closer__sample {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: var(--space-md);
    border: var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--paper-2);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    white-space: pre;
  }

  .closer__quote {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-block: var(--space-md) var(--space-lg);
    padding-left: var(--space-md);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .closer__outro {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
    padding-top: var(--space-lg);
    border-top: var(--border);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    font-weight: var(--weight-medium);
  }
</style>
